<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-proj-R2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Orthogonal projection in <m> \R^2 </m></title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case:
	<ol>

		<li> Compute <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m>. </li>

		<li xml:id="instruction-exercisegroup-exercisegroup-orthog-proj-R2-decompose"><p>
			Express <m> \uvec{u} </m> as a decomposition into a sum of its
			<term><xref ref="definition-orthog-parallel-component" text="custom">vector component parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m></xref></term>
			and its
			<term><xref ref="definition-orthog-orthog-component" text="custom">vector component orthogonal to <m> \uvec{a} </m></xref></term>.
		</p></li>

		<li><p>
			Sketch a diagram of the decomposition from <xref ref="instruction-exercisegroup-exercisegroup-orthog-proj-R2-decompose">part</xref> in the <m> xy </m>-plane,
			with the initial point of <m> \uvec{u} </m> at the origin.
			On your diagram also draw:
			<ul>
				<li> Vector <m> \uvec{a} </m>, with its initial point at the origin. </li>
				<li> The line through the origin parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m>. </li>
			</ul>
		</p></li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{u} = (2,5) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{a} = (2,1) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><m> \uproj{u}{a} = ( 18/5, 9/5) </m></li>

		<li><p>
			<m> \uvec{u} = \uvec{p} + \uvec{n} </m>,
			where <m> \uvec{p} = \uproj{u}{a} </m> as already computed,
			and <md> \uvec{n} = \uvec{u} - \uvec{p} = ( -8/5, 16/5) </md>.
		</p></li>

		<li>
			<image width="45.2%">
				<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-proj-R2-quad1">
					<xi:include href="orthog-proj-R2.d/quad1.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
				<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a decomposition of a vector into a sum of its components parallel to and orthogonal to a specific vector. </shortdescription>
				<description><p>
					A diagram consisting of a right triangle of vectors in the first quadrant of the <m> xy </m>-plane.
					The legs of the right triangle are vector <m> \uvec{p} </m>,
					drawn from the origin to the point <m> ( 18/5, 9/5) </m>,
					and vector <m> \uvec{n} </m>,
					drawn from the terminal point of <m> \uvec{p} </m> to the point <m> (2,5) </m>.
					The hypotenuse of the triangle is vector <m> \uvec{u} </m>,
					drawn from the origin to the point <m> (2,5) </m>.
					A fourth vector <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
					parallel to but shorter than <m> \uvec{p} </m>,
					is drawn from the origin to the point <m> (2, 1) </m>.
					Finally, the line through the origin parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m> and <m> \uvec{p} </m> is drawn and labelled as <m> \ell </m>.
				</p></description>
			</image>
		</li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{u} = (-2,3) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{a} = (5,-1) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><m> \uproj{u}{a} = ( -5/2, 1/2) </m></li>

		<li><p>
			<m> \uvec{u} = \uvec{p} + \uvec{n} </m>,
			where <m> \uvec{p} = \uproj{u}{a} </m> as already computed,
			and <md> \uvec{n} = \uvec{u} - \uvec{p} = ( 1/2, 5/2) </md>.
		</p></li>

		<li>
			<image width="60.8%">
				<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-proj-R2-quad2">
					<xi:include href="orthog-proj-R2.d/quad2.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
				<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a decomposition of a vector into a sum of its components parallel to and orthogonal to a specific vector. </shortdescription>
				<description><p>
					A diagram consisting of a right triangle of vectors in the second quadrant of the <m> xy </m>-plane.
					The legs of the right triangle are vector <m> \uvec{p} </m>,
					drawn from the origin to the point <m> ( -5/2, 1/2) </m>,
					and vector <m> \uvec{n} </m>,
					drawn from the terminal point of <m> \uvec{p} </m> to the point <m> (-2,3) </m>.
					The hypotenuse of the triangle is vector <m> \uvec{u} </m>,
					drawn from the origin to the point <m> (-2,3) </m>.
					A fourth vector <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
					parallel but oppositely directed to <m> \uvec{p} </m>,
					is drawn from the origin to the point <m> (5, -1) </m>.
					Finally, the line through the origin parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m> and <m> \uvec{p} </m> is drawn and labelled as <m> \ell </m>.
				</p></description>
			</image>
		</li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
